  "protocol": "One fresh server per width, sequentially 4, 1, 8. Same 912/64 prompt and greedy reference. Each gets the same 8/8 warmup and normal default prefill. Stock model libraries, separate QKV and Z and ordinary recurrent-state gather. Only the benchmark server requests output capacity equal to the width and supplies consecutive reference tokens on the actual target decode graph. The first output comes from normal prefill and is checked. The remaining 63 positions are teacher-forced at their original positions, requesting all logits. Each block is synchronized and each greedy argmax is compared with the next reference ID. Full-width blocks plus a final short block. No draft model, rollback, CPU or GPU profiler. The 8 GiB managed cap and per-process high-water were checked. OS file cache not controlled.",
  "interpretation": "Target-only throughput divides the 63 positions by summed synchronized target time. Target plus argmax also includes scanning all returned logits. The measured rate assumes every supplied prefix is correct; a mismatch is scored against the serial reference, and the next block still uses the reference. Neither metric includes drafting, rejected tokens, client overhead or rollback. One measurement per width. This brackets the current verifier on this fixture; it is not a general upper bound for new kernels or other widths.",
  "initial_attempt": "The original benchmark process inherited the normal one-output server limit and stopped before completing width 4. No timing from that process is used. The benchmark patch reserves the requested output width at context setup under the same cap.",
